> Anki
# A flash-card program, for memorizing words
# Works with SRS - Spaced Repetition System; optimizes frequency at which words appear for you to learn
! Not a substitute for language-exposure. It helps you understand sentences you come across; doesn't cement your understanding on its own.
* https://www.immersionkit.com/dictionary       # Immersion Kit
* https://jpdb.io/
* https://ankiweb.net/shared/decks              # Pre-Made Decks
* https://ankiweb.net/shared/info/2055492159    # Anki Connect Addon
* Method
*   use a pre-made deck of common 1k-2k words, with about 10 new words every day
*   Use vocab cards for plain nouns, and sentence cards (word within sentence, for context) for nuanced words
*   Switch to FSRS algorithm (a more advanced algorithm, from 2023)
*       Trenton recommends: "Desired retention": 0.85, "New cards/day": 10, "Maximum reviews/day": 9999, "Interday learning/review order": "Mix with reviews", "Leech action": "Tag Only" (if you don't want continuously failed words to be suspended)
*   Go to AnkiWeb and get some popular starter decks  https://ankiweb.net/shared/decks
*       like the "Tango", "paid refold"(?), "Core 2k / 6k", "Kaishi 1.5k" (relatively recent, very easy to understand)
*   Use addons relevant to you
*       Custom Background and Gear Icon (optional)
*       Review Heatmap (github-like activity tracking)
*       Kanji Grid Kuuube (show a heatmap of all the kanji you learned)
*       Image Resizer (define a constant image size for images you save - helps save on space, and makes the quality more consistent)
*       AnkiConnect (works with Yomichan/Yomitan; download a dictionary (and maybe pitch-accent dictionary) to shift+click words for a pop-up menu in browser that lets you create cards)

