>> Important notes on learning Japanese (and languages in general)
> Exposure method (and Listening Comprehension)
# Get a lot of input, mimic what you hear. Repeat constantly.
* Input (exposure to Japanese speech/writing) is much more important than learning syntactic rules
*   Listening is better than reading at least at the beginning - you won't get the intonations right from reading
* For multiple hours a day, listen to podcasts / youtube videos / anime / ... anything in native-Japanese
*   Passive listening (hearing and trying to understand while doing something else, like preparing food) helps!
* Use SRS (Spaced Repetition System), like Anki
*   Learn about 10 words a day. You can do more than that, but it's hardly sustainable.
*   Anki is a powerful too, but not the main one. You need to hear the words you learn in context.
*   Create vocabulary cards of your own. There are a lot of tools to do so
* Learn the sounds before learning to read properly
*   Unless you're really only interested in reading
*   You can wait 6-12 months before learning to read, just focusing on learning pronounciations and intonations. And that's still a bit of a rush into reading.
*       Learn the "pitch accent"
*           You can practice with this site:  https://kotu.io/tests/pitchAccent/minimalPairs
* Use the language in (digital/typing, not manual) writing





